<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Looped transformers and latent CoT.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research has explored how enable a model with a fixed number of layers to think deeper about problems through architectural design or specialized training, effectively simulating a deeper transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. A fundamental strategy in this direction involves loop-based architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al., 2018b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohtashami et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that standard fixed-depth transformers struggle in length generalization which can be significantly improved by looped transformers. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saunshi et al. (2025)</a></cite> first proves that a <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-depth looped transformer can implicitly generate latent thoughts and can simulate <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps of CoT reasoning under mild assumptions. Furthermore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill &amp; Sabharwal (2025)</a></cite> shows that a looped transformer with depth <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> can solve regular language recognition and graph connectivity with arbitrary input context length <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is intractable by LLMs with logarithmic CoT steps.
Mixture of Recursion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is the recent looped transformer that practically works, which adaptively adjust the looping depth for tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to architectural recurrence, which necessitates explicit structural changes, an alternative known as <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous chain-of-thought (continuous CoT)</span> achieves comparable computational advantages through specialized training of standard transformer models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng &amp; Van Durme, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A representative is COCONUT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which operates on the continuous token space instead of using recurrent parameters. COCONUT directly treats the last hidden state of previous tokens as reasoning tokens for CoT reasoning, allowing them to explore multiple reasoning paths simultaneously, akin to breadth-first search, without being constrained to natural language tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gozeten et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Continuous CoT outperforms standard discrete CoT in certain reasoning tasks demanding parallel searching and multiple reasoning paths, yet still falls behind in general tasks.
Notably, latent CoT could be simulated by continuous diffusion with diffusion forcing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and we focus on looped transformer in the main context for clarity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion language models.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion language models emerge as a new paradigm that reformulates text generation as an iterative denoising process, enabling complex reasoning by leveraging full-sequence context. This paradigm primarily includes <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs)</span>, which are a type of DDMs, and <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Embedding-based Diffusion Models (EDMs)</span>, a subset of CDMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">MDMs operate on discrete tokens, starting from a masked sequence and refining tokens simultaneously using bidirectional context. Early foundational work includes D3PM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and SEDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which introduced discrete transition processes and score matching losses. Subsequent methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> streamlined training through hybrid masked losses, facilitating the conversion of encoder models like BERT into generative reasoners. The iterative unmasking process inherent in MDMs supports sophisticated reasoning capabilities, such as iterative refinement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and reverse-order reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The framework has also been integrated with chain-of-thought reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, demonstrating strong performance in tasks requiring parallel context and systematic refinement. Similar algorithms are proposed from the flow matching perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Additional to mask noises, some work try to leverage uniform noises which tend to have worse performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Rütte et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaul et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, EDMs perform diffusion in a continuous embedding space. EDM research focused on controllable generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and sequence-to-sequence tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dieleman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahabadi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, with Plaid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani &amp; Hashimoto, 2023</a>)</cite> later establishing empirical scaling laws that significantly narrowed the efficiency gap with autoregressive models. The framework was further extended by DoT-Plaid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which generalized chain-of-thought reasoning to EDMs, leveraging iterative latent refinement for improved coherence and mathematical reasoning. There are also a few continuous diffusion models operating on the logit space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Some previous work have noticed the potential of multimodal generation integrating continuous and discrete diffusion, with applications to text-image joint generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and protein sequence-structure co-design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. DUO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> tries to connect two types of diffusion models via marginal matching, and apply distillation tricks for continuous diffusion to discrete text diffusion. In comparison, our work generalize their results and provide systematic analysis on expressiveness and trainability, while practically combine continuous and discrete models to benefit each other.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Representation learning for diffusion models.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in representation-enhanced diffusion model training show that high-quality representations from pretrained models could benefit the training efficiency and sampling quality of diffusion models through flexible ways <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kouzelis et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In particular, RCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> and GeoRCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> adopt two-stage generation processes where a representation generator first samples high-level features which serve as the conditions for the second-stage image or molecule generation. In contrast, REPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a training-time technique that aligns the internal features of diffusion models with external pretrained representations, thereby accelerating the training procedure. REED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> unifies RCG and REPA from a theoretical perspective, and generalizes these methods by leveraging multi-modal representations and improved training curriculum. Furthermore, ReDi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kouzelis et al., 2025</a>)</cite> demonstrates that generating images and their representations at the same time also boosts generation quality of diffusion models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Omitted Proof</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Theoretical Expressivity Analysis</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides proof for <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in the main text and presents additional results. We assume standard measurability/Lipschitz conditions when needed.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.1 </span>Continuous Diffusion Dominates Discrete Diffusion</h4>

<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Regularity for continuous diffusion)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We assume <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> on a set of times of positive measure in <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is such that the Fokker–Planck equation is well-posed and yields absolutely continuous marginals for <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> when starting from a distribution with a density or from any point mass convolved with the Gaussian noise of equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Embedded discrete trajectories are finitely supported at each <math id="Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{t}\}_{t\in[0,1]}\in\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{t}\}_{t\in[0,1]}\in\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the embedded marginal <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}:=\mathcal{E}_{\sharp}p_{t}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}:=\mathcal{E}_{\sharp}p_{t}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a <em id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">finite</em> set in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is one-hot or any fixed finite codebook, then <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite mixture of Dirac masses in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability vector over the finite set <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\Omega^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\Omega^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> (size <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{supp}(p_{t})\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{supp}(p_{t})\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. The encoder <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> maps each <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to a single point <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the pushforward <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(B)=p_{t}(\mathcal{E}^{-1}(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(B)=p_{t}(\mathcal{E}^{-1}(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on the finite set <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{E}(x):x\in\mathcal{X}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{E}(x):x\in\mathcal{X}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite atomic measure in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuous diffusion produces absolutely continuous marginals)<span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Regularity for continuous diffusion). ‣ B.1.1 Continuous Diffusion Dominates Discrete Diffusion ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for any <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the marginal <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^{L\times d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^{L\times d})</annotation></semantics></math> arising from equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is absolutely continuous w.r.t. the Lebesgue measure on <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the VP case equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\alpha_{t}z_{0}+\sigma_{t}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\alpha_{t}z_{0}+\sigma_{t}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Gaussian smoothing of the law of <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus absolutely continuous.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">With <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> on a set of positive measure and standard regularity on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fokker–Planck operator is (hypo)elliptic on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from any initial distribution with a density (or from a point mass, which immediately becomes smooth by Gaussian convolution when <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>), the solution <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a density for any <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the VP instance equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine transformation of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> plus independent Gaussian noise with variance <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}I</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the convolution of the law of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with a non-degenerate Gaussian, which is absolutely continuous.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strict trajectory-level gap, <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Strict trajectory-level gap). ‣ Continuous diffusion dominates discrete diffusion. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in main text)<span id="Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At any fixed <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following strict inclusion</span></p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)\ \subsetneq\ \mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)\quad\text{as subsets of }\ \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^{L\times d})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">⊊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">as subsets of </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)\ \subsetneq\ \mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)\quad\text{as subsets of }\ \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^{L\times d})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3 (Embedded discrete trajectories are finitely supported at each 𝑡). ‣ B.1.1 Continuous Diffusion Dominates Discrete Diffusion ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, each <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a finite set in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4 (Continuous diffusion produces absolutely continuous marginals). ‣ B.1.1 Continuous Diffusion Dominates Discrete Diffusion ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, there exist (indeed, generically) <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\in\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\in\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> that are absolutely continuous and thus non-atomic. No absolutely continuous distribution can be a finite atomic measure; hence <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)\subsetneq\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{F}}_{\mathrm{disc}}(\theta)\subsetneq\mathsf{F}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as sets of possible marginals at time <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The strictness holds for any <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> on a set of positive measure before <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, notice that the actual gap may be small, and discrete models with sufficient capacities (such as LLMs) can still approximate distributions pretty well given sufficient vocabulary size, sequence length and training data. Thus in additional to the (marginal) expressivity gain, utilization of continuous representations is also a practical consideration for improving empirical performance: the well-pretrained representations could facilitate the diffusion model training via representation alignment and guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kouzelis et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information loss from token sampling in discrete reverse steps)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>V</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the logits predicted at a discrete reverse step, and let the next input be the sampled token <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t^{-}}\sim\mathrm{Cat}(\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t^{-}}\sim\mathrm{Cat}(\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous random vector with a non-degenerate distribution (e.g., due to data randomness). Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\ell_{\theta};\,x_{t^{-}})\ \leq\ H(x_{t^{-}})\ \leq\ \log V\ &lt;\ H(\ell_{\theta})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\ell_{\theta};\,x_{t^{-}})\ \leq\ H(x_{t^{-}})\ \leq\ \log V\ &lt;\ H(\ell_{\theta}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hence the mapping <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}\mapsto x_{t^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}\mapsto x_{t^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> is information-losing, and the full logit geometry is not preserved along the trajectory.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By data processing inequality for the Markov chain <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}\to\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta})\to x_{t^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}\to\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta})\to x_{t^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> (followed by categorical sampling), <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\ell_{\theta};x_{t^{-}})\leq I(\ell_{\theta};\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta}))\leq H(x_{t^{-}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\ell_{\theta};x_{t^{-}})\leq I(\ell_{\theta};\mathrm{softmax}(\ell_{\theta}))\leq H(x_{t^{-}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes values in a finite set of size <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(x_{t^{-}})\leq\log V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(x_{t^{-}})\leq\log V</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous/non-degenerate, so its (differential) entropy <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\ell_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\ell_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily large, and the discrete entropy <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\lfloor\ell_{\theta}\rfloor)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\lfloor\ell_{\theta}\rfloor)</annotation></semantics></math> is also unbounded with quantization fineness; in particular, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\ell_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\ell_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is not bounded by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log V</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\ell_{\theta};x_{t^{-}})&lt;H(\ell_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\ell_{\theta};x_{t^{-}})&lt;H(\ell_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>; the mapping is many-to-one and loses information about <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> beyond what is encoded in the sampled index.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.2 </span>Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer</h4>

<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mild regularity for numerical integration)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the reverse PF–ODE equation <a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> uses a vector field <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(z,t):=a_{t}(z)-\tfrac{1}{2}g_{t}^{2}s_{\theta}(z,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(z,t):=a_{t}(z)-\tfrac{1}{2}g_{t}^{2}s_{\theta}(z,t)</annotation></semantics></math> that is globally Lipschitz in <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and piecewise continuous in <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and a standard one-step method <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., explicit Euler) that is consistent of order <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuous diffusion sampler can <em id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simulate</em> looped rollouts, Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2 (Continuous diffusion sampler can simulate looped rollouts). ‣ Continuous diffusion generalizes looped transformer. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in main text)<span id="Thmtheorem7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix any looped transformer <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the (deterministic) sampler for the reverse PF–ODE by the explicit Euler method with step size <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/T</annotation></semantics></math> and choose the vector field on grid points by</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{\theta}(z,t_{k})\ :=\ \Phi_{\theta}(z)-z,\qquad k=0,\dots,T-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(z,t_{k})\ :=\ \Phi_{\theta}(z)-z,\qquad k=0,\dots,T-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the sampler update is</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t_{k+1}}\ =\ z_{t_{k}}\ +\ \Delta t\,v_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})\ =\ \Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t_{k+1}}\ =\ z_{t_{k}}\ +\ \Delta t\,v_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})\ =\ \Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which exactly reproduces the looped rollout <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> when we identify <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k}=z_{t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k}=z_{t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1/T</annotation></semantics></math> and the explicit Euler method,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t_{k+1}}=z_{t_{k}}+\Delta t\,v_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})=z_{t_{k}}+\tfrac{1}{T}\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})-z_{t_{k}}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t_{k+1}}=z_{t_{k}}+\Delta t\,v_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})=z_{t_{k}}+\tfrac{1}{T}\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})-z_{t_{k}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">If we instead scale the vector field as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z,t_{k}):=T\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z)-z\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>scaled</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z,t_{k}):=T\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z)-z\big)</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1/T</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t_{k+1}}=z_{t_{k}}+\Delta t\,v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})=z_{t_{k}}+\tfrac{1}{T}\cdot T\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})-z_{t_{k}}\big)=\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>scaled</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t_{k+1}}=z_{t_{k}}+\Delta t\,v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z_{t_{k}},t_{k})=z_{t_{k}}+\tfrac{1}{T}\cdot T\big(\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}})-z_{t_{k}}\big)=\Phi_{\theta}(z_{t_{k}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus each sampler step equals one looped-transformer application. Since the construction uses the same network <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (embedded into <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> through the formula above) and a time index via <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the equality holds step by step.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Looped transformer can emulate diffusion ODE terminal maps with timestep embeddings and residual connections)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the reverse sampler integrate the PF–ODE equation <a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> with a one-step method <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Mild regularity for numerical integration). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Define a looped transformer <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(\cdot;k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ode</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(\cdot;k)</annotation></semantics></math> that, at step <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, applies the numerical increment</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(z;k)\ :=\ \Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})\ =\ z\ +\ \Delta t_{k}\,v_{\theta}(z,t_{k})\ +\ \mathcal{O}(\Delta t_{k}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ode</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.722em" rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(z;k)\ :=\ \Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})\ =\ z\ +\ \Delta t_{k}\,v_{\theta}(z,t_{k})\ +\ \mathcal{O}(\Delta t_{k}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by the same <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (time-conditioned). Then unrolling <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps computes the same discrete trajectory as the ODE sampler up to the integrator’s local truncation error; as <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (mesh size <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>), the terminal error vanishes by standard numerical ODE theory.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">At each step <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the looped transformer block applies the map <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(z,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(h_{k};k)=\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(h_{k},t_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ode</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}^{\mathrm{ode}}(h_{k};k)=\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}(h_{k},t_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">This matches the sampler’s numerical update. The global error after <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps is bounded by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for a Lipschitz vector field (by Grönwall-type stability bounds and order-<math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> consistency). Taking the mesh to zero drives the terminal error to zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stochastic paths and determinism)<span id="Thmremark3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the reverse equation <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> yields a <em id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">distribution over trajectories</em>. A purely deterministic looped rollout <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, given fixed initial <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, cannot match a non-degenerate stochastic path law. If one augments the looped transformer with exogenous randomness (e.g., <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at initialization or fresh per-step noise) and allows conditioning on <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> at each step, terminal distributions can be matched in principle by pushing <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> through the unrolled network.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strictness vs. parity: diffusion vs. looped transformer)<span id="Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the <em id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">same</em> parameter budget and the “single time-conditioned network” protocol:</span></p>
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(</span><em id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">Deterministic ODE samplers</em><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) If the reverse uses the PF–ODE (equation </span><a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Continuous diffusion. ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) and is implemented by a standard one-step method, then continuous diffusion is </span><em id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph">not</em><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> strictly more expressive in terms of terminal distributions: by Propositions </span><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2 (Continuous diffusion sampler can simulate looped rollouts). ‣ Continuous diffusion generalizes looped transformer. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8 (Looped transformer can emulate diffusion ODE terminal maps with timestep embeddings and residual connections). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, each can simulate the other’s discrete rollout (up to vanishing numerical error).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(</span><em id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">Stochastic path laws</em><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) If </span><math id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the looped transformer is deterministic (no exogenous noise), continuous diffusion is </span><em id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph">strictly</em><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> more expressive at the </span><em id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph">trajectory</em><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> level (cannot match the non-degenerate stochastic path law with a deterministic map).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2 (Continuous diffusion sampler can simulate looped rollouts). ‣ Continuous diffusion generalizes looped transformer. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows a diffusion ODE sampler with Euler steps can exactly recover a looped rollout (by choosing <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z,t_{k})=T(\Phi_{\theta}(z)-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>scaled</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}^{(\mathrm{scaled})}(z,t_{k})=T(\Phi_{\theta}(z)-z)</annotation></semantics></math>). Conversely, Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8 (Looped transformer can emulate diffusion ODE terminal maps with timestep embeddings and residual connections). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows a looped transformer can emulate the numerical ODE integrator step map <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\Delta t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>; standard numerical analysis ensures convergence of the terminal state as the mesh refines.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">(ii) Suppose the diffusion reverse is an SDE with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> on a set of positive measure. Then the terminal random variable <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has a non-degenerate conditional distribution given <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (and the Brownian path). A deterministic looped rollout <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k+1}=\Phi_{\theta}(h_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a measurable function of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> only and thus produces a Dirac path law; it cannot match a non-degenerate distribution over trajectories. Hence diffusion is strictly more expressive pathwise.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, if the looped model is allowed an auxiliary noise input <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> (initial or per-step) and time conditioning, then the unrolled map can be written as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{T}=\Gamma_{\theta}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{T}=\Gamma_{\theta}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> for some measurable <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any target terminal law with a Borel probability measure on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a pushforward of a simple noise (e.g., Gaussian) that realizes it; with sufficient capacity, the looped model can approximate such a map. Thus terminal parity is achievable in principle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Timestep embeddings and intermediate supervision)<span id="Thmremark4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>, timestep embeddings provably improve the expressiveness of looped transformers, though the gain might be marginal in practice.
Supervising intermediate diffusion steps (multi-<math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> losses) does not change the representable set either; it improves optimization and inductive bias. Stochastic SDE sampling (<math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) enlarges the <em id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">path</em> distribution class (Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3 (Stochastic paths and determinism). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) but does not imply a strict advantage on terminal laws once exogenous noise is also allowed in looped rollouts.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this subsection (corresponding to <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in the main text) by summarizing the comparison in expressivity as follows.</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discrete vs. Continuous Diffusion:</span> By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Strict trajectory-level gap). ‣ Continuous diffusion dominates discrete diffusion. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, continuous diffusion strictly dominates discrete diffusion at the <em id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trajectory</em> level in <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> (non-atomic intermediate marginals vs. finite-atomic). Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 6 (Information loss from token sampling in discrete reverse steps). ‣ B.1.1 Continuous Diffusion Dominates Discrete Diffusion ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows discrete per-step token sampling loses full logit information, whereas continuous samplers preserve all coordinates without mandatory quantization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continuous Diffusion vs. Looped Transformer:</span> Continuous diffusion samplers can <em id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simulate</em> looped rollouts (Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2 (Continuous diffusion sampler can simulate looped rollouts). ‣ Continuous diffusion generalizes looped transformer. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ 3 Theoretical Expressivity and Practical Trainability Analysis ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>); looped rollouts can <em id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">emulate</em> ODE samplers (Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8 (Looped transformer can emulate diffusion ODE terminal maps with timestep embeddings and residual connections). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Diffusion with <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly more expressive <em id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pathwise</em> than deterministic looped rollouts (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9 (Strictness vs. parity: diffusion vs. looped transformer). ‣ B.1.2 Continuous Diffusion Generalizes Looped Transformer ‣ B.1 Theoretical Expressivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>(ii)).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Analysis of CCDD</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection corresponds to <a href="#S4" title="4 Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the main text, providing theoretical results and more analysis of CCDD.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.1 </span>Expressivity of CCDD</h4>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expressivity comparison.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now compare the joint model against (i) <em id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continuous diffusion alone</em> (on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>) and (ii) choices of reverse parameterization.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Expressivity vs. continuous-only diffusion: joint law and marginals)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the hypothesis classes of terminal laws <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}:=\{\,\text{laws of }(x_{0},z_{0})\text{ produced by the joint model}\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">laws of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> produced by the joint model</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}:=\{\,\text{laws of }(x_{0},z_{0})\text{ produced by the joint model}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cont}}:=\{\,\text{laws of }z_{0}\text{ produced by continuous diffusion alone}\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">laws of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> produced by continuous diffusion alone</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cont}}:=\{\,\text{laws of }z_{0}\text{ produced by continuous diffusion alone}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then:</span></p>
<ol id="A2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On the </span><em id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">joint space</em><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><math id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> strictly extends continuous-only modeling (which does not produce </span><math id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> at all); i.e., the joint model is strictly more expressive if the target includes the discrete marginal.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On the </span><em id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">continuous marginal</em><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> alone, the joint model does </span><em id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph">not</em><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> enlarge the class of </span><math id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> laws beyond a continuous diffusion with the same parameter budget and time-conditioning: for any </span><math id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, its </span><math id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-marginal </span><math id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> lies in </span><math id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I3.ix2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (up to decoder equivalence).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) Trivial: continuous-only models do not define a distribution on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; the joint model does.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(ii) Let <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{joint}}</annotation></semantics></math> be realized by some reverse parameterization with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a continuous-only model with inputs <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> whose network computes the same <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-head as the joint model but with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> embedded by a deterministic encoder <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> predicted from <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> (this is implementable because the joint reverse factor <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> only through a measurable function). Universal approximation guarantees allow the continuous-only network to approximate the composite map <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(z_{t},t)\mapsto p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{cont}}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{t},t)\mapsto p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{cont}}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> after marginalizing out <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(x_{t}\mid z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(x_{t}\mid z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (which the network can input as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\phi(x_{t})\mid z_{t},t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\phi(x_{t})\mid z_{t},t]</annotation></semantics></math> for a rich feature dictionary <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore the induced terminal <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-law can be matched. The decoder equivalence argument is standard: terminal classification/regression heads can implement the same marginal law.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10 (Expressivity vs. continuous-only diffusion: joint law and marginals). ‣ Expressivity comparison. ‣ B.2.1 Expressivity of CCDD ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>(ii) states a <em id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">marginal</em> parity: adding a discrete companion does not expand the set of achievable <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-marginals at the level of function classes (with time-conditioning and equal total parameters). Practically it may ease optimization by providing an auxiliary target.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Semigroup structure and sufficient expressivity of factored parameterization.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the joint forward marginal induced by equation <a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Forward process. ‣ 4.1 Joint Continuous-Discrete Diffusion ‣ 4 Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. Then</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(x,z)\;=\;\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x),\qquad\Delta_{z}\log q_{t}(x,z)\;=\;\Delta_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(x,z)\;=\;\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x),\qquad\Delta_{z}\log q_{t}(x,z)\;=\;\Delta_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">i.e., the continuous score depends on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only through</em> the conditional <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(z\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(z\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the discrete part, Bayes posteriors use <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(x\mid z)\propto q_{t}(x)\,q_{t}(z\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(x\mid z)\propto q_{t}(x)\,q_{t}(z\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Forward semigroup and Trotter factorization)<span id="Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{t}^{(z)}\}_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{t}^{(z)}\}_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{t}^{(x)}\}_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{t}^{(x)}\}_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Markov semigroups generated by the continuous FP operator <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}^{(z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}^{(z)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the discrete generator <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> (Kolmogorov forward) respectively, both time-inhomogeneous but piecewise constant in small intervals. Then the joint forward semigroup on <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> with <em id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">independent</em> corruption is <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}=T_{t}^{(x)}T_{t}^{(z)}=T_{t}^{(z)}T_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}=T_{t}^{(x)}T_{t}^{(z)}=T_{t}^{(z)}T_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> and, for a partition <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=t_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;t_{N}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=t_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;t_{N}=t</annotation></semantics></math> with mesh <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}\Delta t_{k}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}\ =\ \lim_{\max\Delta t_{k}\to 0}\ \prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big(T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(x)}\,T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(z)}\Big)\ =\ \lim_{\max\Delta t_{k}\to 0}\ \prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big(T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(z)}\,T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(x)}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.500em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}\ =\ \lim_{\max\Delta t_{k}\to 0}\ \prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big(T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(x)}\,T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(z)}\Big)\ =\ \lim_{\max\Delta t_{k}\to 0}\ \prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big(T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(z)}\,T_{\Delta t_{k}}^{(x)}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Independence in equation <a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Forward process. ‣ 4.1 Joint Continuous-Discrete Diffusion ‣ 4 Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> implies that the joint generator is the <em id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sum</em> <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}=L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}=L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on functions <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{t}f)(x,z)=(L_{t}^{(z)}f)(x,z)+(L_{t}^{(x)}f)(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{t}f)(x,z)=(L_{t}^{(z)}f)(x,z)+(L_{t}^{(x)}f)(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math>. For (piecewise) time-constant generators the Chernoff–Lie–Trotter product formula yields the stated limits; commutativity at the semigroup level follows from independence.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Effect of factored reverse kernels)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a time step <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to s</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the family of joint reverse kernels <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\{\,K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\{\,K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{fact}}\ :=\ \Big\{\,K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})=K_{x}(x_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,K_{z}(z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,\Big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>fact</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{fact}}\ :=\ \Big\{\,K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})=K_{x}(x_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,K_{z}(z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})\,\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the <em id="Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">factored</em> family in equation <a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Reverse process. ‣ 4.1 Joint Continuous-Discrete Diffusion ‣ 4 Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. Then:</span></p>
<ol id="A2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">Single-step limitation.</em><span id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><math id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{fact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>fact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{fact}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a strict subset of </span><math id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: there exist joint kernels with within-step couplings that cannot be written as a product independent of </span><math id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{s},z_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{s},z_{s})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> cross-dependence.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">Splitting sufficiency (small steps).</em><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Suppose the target joint dynamics has generator </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}=L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}=L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (no cross-diffusion term), and the factored reverse uses </span><em id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph">conditionally coupled</em><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> factors </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{x}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{z}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{z}(\cdot\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then, by Lie–Trotter splitting, iterating factored kernels at a small step size </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><em id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph">alternately</em><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (e.g., </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{z}K_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{z}K_{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> per micro-step) converges to the </span><em id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_emph">same</em><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> joint semigroup as any coupled kernel generated by </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, hence there is no expressivity loss at the trajectory level as </span><math id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I4.ix2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(a) Consider <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a joint kernel that enforces <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{s}=\bm{1}\{z_{s}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}=\bm{1}\{z_{s}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely (hard constraint). This coupling cannot be expressed as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{x}(x_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})K_{z}(z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x}(x_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})K_{z}(z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> because any factorization leaves <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">given</em> <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the deterministic relation between <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">(b) Under the assumed generator sum <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact joint semigroup over a small interval <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\Delta t(L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\Delta t(L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)})}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the alternating product <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\Delta tL_{t}^{(z)}}e^{\Delta tL_{t}^{(x)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\Delta tL_{t}^{(z)}}e^{\Delta tL_{t}^{(x)}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or the corresponding Markov kernels <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{z}K_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{z}K_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) approximates it with first-order error <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\Delta t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\Delta t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Over a partition with mesh <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\Delta t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\Delta t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the product converges to the exact semigroup (Chernoff–Lie–Trotter), hence the factored parameterization with alternating micro-steps is sufficient to realize the same family of joint laws.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Coupling via conditioning is enough in the small-step limit)<span id="Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Even though <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x_{s},z_{s}\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> does not condition on <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{s},z_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{s},z_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> jointly in one shot, allowing each factor to depend on <em id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">both</em> <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and alternating the updates yields asymptotically the same expressivity as a fully coupled kernel driven by a sum-generator <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}^{(z)}+L_{t}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the diffusion process design of our language modeling task is flexible as long as it preserve the clean marginals, thus a factored process suffices.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6</span></span><span id="Thmremark6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(When factorization truly hurts)<span id="Thmremark6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the target joint generator includes <em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">cross terms</em> that cannot be written as <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{(z)}+L^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{(z)}+L^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a diffusion on <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> whose drift or diffusion matrix depends on <em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">future</em> <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, or a discrete jump rate at time <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>), then any per-step factorization that does not see <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{s},z_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{s},z_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> jointly will <em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">not</em> reproduce such dynamics without further inner iterations (e.g., Gibbs-within-step to sample <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning on <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, we can always construct a proper diffusion process without cross-terms as in the main text (as long as it preserve the terminal marginals), hence the factored parameterization is expressive enough for our language generation task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.2 </span>Designs of Schedules</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide more discussions on the design space of noise schedules in CCDD.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Information theory perspective.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the tools in information theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mutual information decay under factored corruption)<span id="Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}:=I((x_{0},z_{0});(x_{t},z_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}:=I((x_{0},z_{0});(x_{t},z_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mutual information between data and the corrupted pair at time <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Under equation <a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Forward process. ‣ 4.1 Joint Continuous-Discrete Diffusion ‣ 4 Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> with independent noises, <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing and satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}I_{t}\ =\ \mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\frac{d}{dt}\log\frac{q_{t}(x_{t},z_{t}\mid x_{0},z_{0})}{q_{t}(x_{t},z_{t})}\,\right]\ \leq\ 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}I_{t}\ =\ \mathbb{E}\!\left[\,\frac{d}{dt}\log\frac{q_{t}(x_{t},z_{t}\mid x_{0},z_{0})}{q_{t}(x_{t},z_{t})}\,\right]\ \leq\ 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with decomposition</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}I_{t}\ =\ \frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ +\ \frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});x_{t}\mid z_{t}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}I_{t}\ =\ \frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ +\ \frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});x_{t}\mid z_{t}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for the VP SDE the continuous contribution equals the negative <em id="Thmtheorem14.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Fisher score power</em>:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ =\ -\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|g_{t}\,\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z_{t}\mid x_{t})\|^{2}\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}I\big((x_{0},z_{0});z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ =\ -\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|g_{t}\,\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z_{t}\mid x_{t})\|^{2}\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and for the CTMC the discrete contribution equals (minus) a <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence–based entropy production rate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Data processing along a Markov chain <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{0},z_{0})\to(x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0},z_{0})\to(x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> yields non-increasing mutual information. The derivative formula follows from differentiating the KL defining mutual information and the Kolmogorov forward equations; for diffusions the de Bruijn–Fisher identity gives the continuous term; for CTMCs one uses the standard entropy production formula <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}H(p_{t})=-\sum_{i\neq j}p_{t}(j)(G_{t})_{ij}\log\frac{p_{t}(i)}{p_{t}(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}H(p_{t})=-\sum_{i\neq j}p_{t}(j)(G_{t})_{ij}\log\frac{p_{t}(i)}{p_{t}(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> representation of conditional MI rates.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">“Optimal” coupling via Hyperparameter Matching.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is natural to have the question: is there an optimal way to couple the two modalities? We discuss the choice of forward hyperparameters (diffusion rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, jump rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>) and their coupling.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Signal-to-noise and posterior conditioning)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the continuous SNR at time <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t):=\alpha_{t}^{2}/\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t):=\alpha_{t}^{2}/\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (VP case), and the discrete SNR by the interpolation weight <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(x_{t}\mid x_{0})=\mathrm{Cat}(\eta_{t}x_{0}+(1-\eta_{t})\pi_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(x_{t}\mid x_{0})=\mathrm{Cat}(\eta_{t}x_{0}+(1-\eta_{t})\pi_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (USDM/masked). Define the <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">joint conditioning strength</em></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}\ :=\ I\big((x_{0},z_{0});\ z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ +\ I\big((x_{0},z_{0});\ x_{t}\mid z_{t}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}\ :=\ I\big((x_{0},z_{0});\ z_{t}\mid x_{t}\big)\ +\ I\big((x_{0},z_{0});\ x_{t}\mid z_{t}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which quantifies how informative each modality remains about its clean counterpart when conditioning on the other.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Entropy/MI matching heuristic)<span id="Thmtheorem15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Informal)
Let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be chosen so that the <em id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">rates</em> of MI decay from Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 14 (Mutual information decay under factored corruption). ‣ Information theory perspective. ‣ B.2.2 Designs of Schedules ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> are balanced:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\big[\|g_{t}\,\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z_{t}\mid x_{t})\|^{2}\big]\ \approx\ \mathrm{Rate}_{x}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Rate</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\big[\|g_{t}\,\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z_{t}\mid x_{t})\|^{2}\big]\ \approx\ \mathrm{Rate}_{x}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Rate}_{x}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Rate</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Rate}_{x}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the CTMC’s conditional MI decay rate (a <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-type quantity). Then the two posteriors <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(z_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> maintain comparable <em id="Thmtheorem15.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">conditioning difficulty</em> (well-conditioned Bayes factors), which in turn stabilizes training losses for heads in the discrete modality.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, training both heads amounts to estimating <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(z_{0}\mid z_{t},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z_{0}\mid z_{t},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{0}\mid x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. If one modality loses information much faster (e.g., <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{z}\ll\mathrm{SNR}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{z}\ll\mathrm{SNR}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>), then one posterior becomes broad/ill-conditioned relative to the other, causing gradient scale mismatch. Balancing the decay rates equalizes expected Fisher information in the continuous head and the discrete information production, yielding comparable curvature of the objectives (via de Bruijn/Fisher for <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and entropy production for the CTMC). This is a standard preconditioning argument based on matching Fisher blocks across modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Schedule matching guideline)<span id="Thmtheorem16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A practical rule is to pick <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <em id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">monotone</em> functions <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\eta_{t}/(1-\eta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\eta_{t}/(1-\eta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> have similar slopes on a log-scale, e.g.,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\log\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t)\ \approx\ \frac{d}{dt}\log\frac{\eta_{t}}{1-\eta_{t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\log\mathrm{SNR}_{z}(t)\ \approx\ \frac{d}{dt}\log\frac{\eta_{t}}{1-\eta_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This equates the relative shrinkage of posterior variances (continuous) and odds (discrete), approximately stabilizing Bayes updates in <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical schedule designs.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From a practical perspective, we could regard the continuous representations as the guidance for the discrete part, the continuous schedule then should be ahead of the discrete schedule (i.e., generated earlier in the reverse process) as useful high-level guidance. This intuition is also validated by other work involving multiple modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geffner et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We adopt this strategy in most experiments, featuring linear schedule for discrete part (<math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}=1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}=1-t</annotation></semantics></math>) and concave schedule for continuous part (<math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\sqrt{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\sqrt{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this subsection by summarizing takeaway messages on CCDD.</p>
<ul id="A2.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formulation.</span> The joint model formalizes a mixed SDE–CTMC system with independent forward corruptions and a reverse denoiser that <em id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conditions</em> on both <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> but factors the per-step kernel across modalities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expressivity vs. continuous-only.</span> Joint modeling is strictly more expressive on the <em id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint space</em>; on the <em id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-marginal</em>, it does not enlarge the class beyond continuous diffusion with comparable capacity (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10 (Expressivity vs. continuous-only diffusion: joint law and marginals). ‣ Expressivity comparison. ‣ B.2.1 Expressivity of CCDD ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of factorization.</span> A single-step factored kernel cannot represent arbitrary within-step couplings (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Effect of factored reverse kernels). ‣ Semigroup structure and sufficient expressivity of factored parameterization. ‣ B.2.1 Expressivity of CCDD ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>(a)), but alternating factored updates at small step sizes attains the same semigroup when the target generator splits (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Effect of factored reverse kernels). ‣ Semigroup structure and sufficient expressivity of factored parameterization. ‣ B.2.1 Expressivity of CCDD ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>(b), Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary 13 (Coupling via conditioning is enough in the small-step limit). ‣ Semigroup structure and sufficient expressivity of factored parameterization. ‣ B.2.1 Expressivity of CCDD ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coupling/schedules.</span> Matching information decay across modalities (Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Proposition 15 (Entropy/MI matching heuristic). ‣ “Optimal” coupling via Hyperparameter Matching. ‣ B.2.2 Designs of Schedules ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Corollary 16 (Schedule matching guideline). ‣ “Optimal” coupling via Hyperparameter Matching. ‣ B.2.2 Designs of Schedules ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) yields well-conditioned posteriors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I5.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Information-theoretic lens.</span> The continuous score is <math id="A2.I5.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\log q_{t}(z\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>; MI decays additively across modalities under independence (Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 14 (Mutual information decay under factored corruption). ‣ Information theory perspective. ‣ B.2.2 Designs of Schedules ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Balancing decay rates aligns Fisher/entropy production and stabilizes learning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> CCDD Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                  
                  
                 Dataset <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> of pairs <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{0},z_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0},z_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}=\mathcal{E}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}=\mathcal{E}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>; time sampler <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathcal{U}([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathcal{U}([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> or discrete grid <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; VP schedule <math id="alg1.l0.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{t},\sigma_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{t},\sigma_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; discrete schedule <math id="alg1.l0.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and noise distribution <math id="alg1.l0.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pi_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; loss weights <math id="alg1.l0.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{cont}}(t),\lambda_{\mathrm{disc}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{cont}}(t),\lambda_{\mathrm{disc}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                  
                  
                 Initialize parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> of a single time-conditioned model <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with two heads:

<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d},\qquad\ell_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times V}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d},\qquad\ell_{\theta}(x_{t},z_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times V}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                  
                  
                 <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each minibatch <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{0}^{(b)},z_{0}^{(b)})\}_{b=1}^{B}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{0}^{(b)},z_{0}^{(b)})\}_{b=1}^{B}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                  
                  
                  Sample times <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t^{(b)}\}_{b=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t^{(b)}\}_{b=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward corruption:</span> Sample <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(b)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(b)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and set

<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}^{(b)}\leftarrow\alpha_{t^{(b)}}\,z_{0}^{(b)}+\sigma_{t^{(b)}}\,\epsilon^{(b)},\quad x_{t}^{(b)}\sim\mathrm{Cat}\!\Big(\eta_{t^{(b)}}\,e_{x_{0}^{(b)}}+(1-\eta_{t^{(b)}})\,\pi_{t^{(b)}}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.506em;" width="1.506em"><mi>Cat</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}^{(b)}\leftarrow\alpha_{t^{(b)}}\,z_{0}^{(b)}+\sigma_{t^{(b)}}\,\epsilon^{(b)},\quad x_{t}^{(b)}\sim\mathrm{Cat}\!\Big(\eta_{t^{(b)}}\,e_{x_{0}^{(b)}}+(1-\eta_{t^{(b)}})\,\pi_{t^{(b)}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model prediction:</span>

<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}^{(b)}\leftarrow\epsilon_{\theta}\!\big(x_{t}^{(b)},\,z_{t}^{(b)},\,t^{(b)}\big),\qquad\ell_{\theta}^{(b)}\leftarrow\ell_{\theta}\!\big(x_{t}^{(b)},\,z_{t}^{(b)},\,t^{(b)}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}^{(b)}\leftarrow\epsilon_{\theta}\!\big(x_{t}^{(b)},\,z_{t}^{(b)},\,t^{(b)}\big),\qquad\ell_{\theta}^{(b)}\leftarrow\ell_{\theta}\!\big(x_{t}^{(b)},\,z_{t}^{(b)},\,t^{(b)}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continuous loss (VP, <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-pred MSE) and discrete loss (token CE on masked tokens):</span>

<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cont}}\;\leftarrow\;\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\lambda_{\mathrm{cont}}(t^{(b)})\,\big\|\epsilon^{(b)}-\epsilon_{\theta}^{(b)}\big\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cont}}\;\leftarrow\;\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\lambda_{\mathrm{cont}}(t^{(b)})\,\big\|\epsilon^{(b)}-\epsilon_{\theta}^{(b)}\big\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{disc}}\;\leftarrow\;-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\lambda_{\mathrm{disc}}(t^{(b)},x_{0}^{(b)},x_{t}^{(b)})\,\log\mathrm{softmax}\!\big(\ell_{\theta}^{(b)}\big)[x_{0}^{(b)}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.058em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{disc}}\;\leftarrow\;-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\lambda_{\mathrm{disc}}(t^{(b)},x_{0}^{(b)},x_{t}^{(b)})\,\log\mathrm{softmax}\!\big(\ell_{\theta}^{(b)}\big)[x_{0}^{(b)}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total loss and update:</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\gamma_{\mathrm{cont}}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cont}}+\gamma_{\mathrm{disc}}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{disc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\gamma_{\mathrm{cont}}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cont}}+\gamma_{\mathrm{disc}}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{disc}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                  
                  
                  Update <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\theta-\eta\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\theta-\eta\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                  
                  
                 <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Algorithm</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now give the algorithmic description of CCDD training in <a href="#alg1" title="In Practical schedule designs. ‣ B.2.2 Designs of Schedules ‣ B.2 Analysis of CCDD ‣ Appendix B Omitted Proof ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and sampling in <a href="#alg2" title="In C.1 Algorithm ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Coevolutionary Continuous Discrete Diffusion: Make Your Diffusion Language Model a Latent Reasoner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using DDPM / DDIM for the continuous example.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> CCDD Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             Time grid <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=t_{0}&gt;t_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;t_{K}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=t_{0}&gt;t_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;t_{K}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; forward schedules <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{t_{k}},\sigma_{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{t_{k}},\sigma_{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{t_{k}},\pi_{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{t_{k}},\pi_{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>; reverse kernels <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t_{k}|t_{k+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t_{k}|t_{k+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for discrete; DDPM/DDIM choice for continuous with variance knob <math id="alg2.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{ddpm}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>ddpm</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{ddpm}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Init:</span> <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t_{0}}\sim\pi_{t_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t_{0}}\sim\pi_{t_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> (uniform or [MASK] prior per token);  <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t_{0}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t_{0}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
